            "text": "{\n  \"persuasion\": \"PERSUADED\",\n  \"support_scope\": \"FULL\",\n  \"rationale\": \"The book defines righteousness substantively as 'right-living' (God's character and the commandments lived out), rather than solely as a forensic mechanism (imputation) or a vague ethical standing. This precisely matches explicit definitions in Deuteronomy 6:25 ('our righteousness, if we observe to do all these commandments'), Psalm 119:172 ('all thy commandments are righteousness'), and 1 John 3:7 ('he that doeth righteousness is righteous'). The strongest counter-argument relies on Pauline texts emphasizing imputed righteousness apart from works (Rom 4, Phil 3). However, the book accounts for these by distinguishing the substance of righteousness (right-living) from the economic claim of how it is acquired (a gift credited to faith vs. a wage owed for labor). The counter-reading requires fracturing the definition of righteousness into separate 'forensic/imputed' and 'moral/experiential' categories to accommodate both Paul and 1 John/Deuteronomy. The book's core identification provides a single, unified definition that fits all cited verses natively.\",\n  \"book_core_identification\": \"Righteousness is substantively defined as right-living—God's own character and, for man, His commandments lived out.\",\n  \"book_secondary_elaborations\": [\n    \"God is Right-Living by definition\",\n    \"Opposite: Sin\"\n  ],\n  \"unsupported_glossary_assertions\": [],\n  \"chapter_only_objections\": [\n    \"The chapter's broader argument that 'works of the law' in Paul specifically targets the economic claim of working for a wage, or turning boundary markers into purchase prices, is a massive theological harmonization that goes beyond simply defining the word 'righteousness'.\",\n    \"The typological identification of the wedding garment in Matthew 22 exclusively as plural 'righteous acts'.\"\n  ],\n  \"strongest_counter_interpretation\": \"Righteousness in the New Testament (especially in Pauline salvation contexts) primarily denotes a legal, forensic standing or status of vindication before God (imputed or credited), fundamentally distinct from the believer's actual moral conduct or commandment-keeping.\",\n  \"counter_core_identification\": \"A forensic legal status or standing granted by God, distinct from the substance of right-living or commandment-keeping.\",\n  \"counter_relation_to_book_core\": \"CONTRADICTS\",\n  \"material_core_denial\": \"The counter asserts that righteousness (in its primary soteriological sense) is a legal status rather than the actual conduct of right-living or commandments lived out.\",\n  \"counter_case\": [\n    \"Romans 4:4-8 emphasizes righteousness being imputed 'without works', setting righteousness in contrast to the law or things done.\",\n    \"Philippians 3:9 contrasts one's 'own righteousness, which is of the law' with 'the righteousness which is of God by faith', suggesting saving righteousness is a status received, not the commandments lived out.\"\n  ],\n  \"book_explanatory_advantages\": [\n    \"Unifies the explicit scriptural definitions of the term (Deut 6:25, Ps 119:172, 1 John 3:7) without needing to invent separate 'forensic' and 'experiential' definitions for the same word.\",\n    \"Explains 1 John 3:7 natively: doing righteousness makes one righteous, directly equating the status with the conduct.\",\n    \"Accounts for God's righteousness (Deut 32:4) and man's righteousness under one conceptual umbrella (right-living).\"\n  ],\n  \"counter_explanatory_advantages\": [\n    \"Directly maps to the dominant Protestant systematic theological category of justification (forensic) as wholly separated from sanctification (experiential).\"\n  ],\n  \"evidence_book_core_cannot_explain\": [],\n  \"comparative_winner\": \"BOOK\",\n  \"decisive_sources\": [\n    \"Deuteronomy 6:25\",\n    \"Psalms 119:172\",\n    \"1 John 3:7\"\n  ],\n  \"unresolved_objections\": [],\n  \"confidence\": \"high\"\n}",
